WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.000
A shelter can look perfectly positioned at noon

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.467
and miss the queue later.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.900
Kirosho helps facilities and event planners
use the shelters they already own,

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.967
around the hours people need shade.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:16.467
This is a fictional Singapore school courtyard,
shown in the SPECS simulator.

00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:19.300
Two shelters serve three activities:

00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.533
lunch, outdoor seating and afternoon pickup.

00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:26.400
The timeline shows projected shade moving from noon to five.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.400
Each activity has its own schedule and analysis height.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.300
The score combines the fraction of its area shaded

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.500
with the number of people present and how long they stay.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.933
I can move either shelter by pinching its handle.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.467
Valid placements snap to the grid.

00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:46.533
The model rejects overlaps, courtyard boundary violations,

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:49.800
and shelters inside the marked access corridor.

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.933
Find better placement evaluates all

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:56.667
5,436 permitted layouts.

00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.900
It proposes the best evaluated grid arrangement

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.667
and waits for a decision.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.900
Here, modeled coverage rises from 32.1%

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.100
to 62.3%: a gain of 30.2 percentage points.

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:15.333
But the lunch queue loses all its modeled whole-period coverage.

00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:17.800
Seating and pickup gain shade.

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.300
That loss is visible before Apply.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.733
The planner can reject the suggestion,

00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:27.167
change the activity profile, or undo an applied layout.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.533
After applying, I inspect the same plan at full scale,

00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:35.600
return to the tabletop and commit a reproducible summary

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.467
with its inputs and layout.

00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:42.133
The spatial view connects each score to the places people use,

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:45.867
making the recommendation and its tradeoffs easier to review.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.100
I planned Kirosho with Devpost Learn,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.467
then used Codex, Snap's CLAD,

00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:55.133
and Lens Studio MCP to build the actual Lens.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.400
Fourteen numerical checks verify its calculations and behavior.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.067
This offline prototype uses illustrative occupancy

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:04.933
and validated solar fixtures.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:08.267
It has not been field-tested or tested on physical glasses.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.367
It does not predict cooling, structural safety

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:13.067
or carbon savings.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.367
Kirosho helps planners make a practical climate-adaptation choice

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.267
before moving real shelters.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.767
Where can our existing shade serve our schedule better?
